WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.737 --> 00:00:14.465
So now I'd like to introduce
today's speaker, Michael McGehee.

00:00:14.465 --> 00:00:17.060
Michael McGehee is a professor
of materials science and

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.048
engineering at Stanford University, and

00:00:19.048 --> 00:00:23.041
the co-director of our Energy Innovation
and Emerging Technologies certificate.

00:00:23.041 --> 00:00:26.735
Which is offered through the Stanford
Center for Professional Development.

00:00:26.735 --> 00:00:29.515
His primary research interests
are developing new materials for

00:00:29.515 --> 00:00:30.975
smart windows and solar cells.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:36.320
He's taught courses on nanotechnology,
organic semiconductors, solar cells, and

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:37.800
polymer science.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.320
He received his undergraduate degree
in physics from Princeton University.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.210
And his PhD was in Materials Science
from the University of

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.340
California at Santa Barbara.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:50.390
There he did research on polymer lasers
in the lab of Nobel laureate Alan Heeger.

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.625
Michael has strong industry
connections acting

00:00:54.625 --> 00:00:58.012
as a technical advisor to Next Energy,
PLANT PV and Sinovia.

00:00:58.012 --> 00:00:59.904
And from his teaching and mentorship,

00:00:59.904 --> 00:01:02.208
he has an impressive crowd
of alumni in his lab.

00:01:02.208 --> 00:01:06.333
Many are now working in Silicon Valley
startups, nine of which have

00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:10.601
their own companies, and four receiving
the Forbes' 30 under 30 award.

00:01:10.601 --> 00:01:12.860
I'd now like to turn
the floor over to Michael.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:15.486
&gt;&gt; Hello everyone.

00:01:15.486 --> 00:01:18.180
Thank you for
participating in this webinar.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:24.220
I'm going to bring you up to speed
on some exciting developments with

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:29.090
Perovskite semiconductors and their use
in solar cells, but to get started,

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:33.390
let me just say a couple things
about the solar industry in general.

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:39.310
In this graph here,
if you look at the orange curve,

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:46.220
you can see that back during the oil
crisis in the 70's solar panels cost

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:52.400
about $100 per watt of power that
they could generate in peak sunlight.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.090
And then that dropped rapidly and
then stagnated

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:01.380
during the 90's when there wasn't
a whole lot of interest in solar energy.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:08.474
And then in the last five or six years,
the price has dived down again and

00:02:08.474 --> 00:02:13.098
now it's at about 50 or 60 cents per watt.

00:02:13.098 --> 00:02:18.548
And with that price reduction,
if you look at the blue curve

00:02:18.548 --> 00:02:24.110
you can see the installations
are rapidly rising with time.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:30.170
And now about 65,000 megawatts
are being installed per year.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:36.330
And that's about the equivalent
of 65 coal fired power plants.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.117
So this is very, very exciting.

00:02:39.117 --> 00:02:42.606
And even more broadly,
I would say that in the last year or

00:02:42.606 --> 00:02:47.430
two we've reached a tipping point
on addressing climate change.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:53.618
And a lot of people are getting
onboard ranging from politicians

00:02:53.618 --> 00:02:59.921
to the utility companies and
I think there's a commitment now and

00:02:59.921 --> 00:03:04.632
that renewable energy is
likely to do very well.

00:03:04.632 --> 00:03:09.469
So solar still needs to get
a little bit better if the overall

00:03:09.469 --> 00:03:14.223
cost of an installed system was
a bout one dollar per watt.

00:03:14.223 --> 00:03:19.265
Then solar electricity would be about
the same cost as electricity from

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:24.820
a gas fired power plant, and here I've
only shown you the price for the panel.

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:29.410
Now installing the panel
is even more expensive.

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:34.400
And at a utility scale that
can be maybe $1.50 per watt.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:40.300
And if you did it on a house,
it might be something like $3.5 per watt.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:45.160
Now today, I'm going to be focusing
more on the solar cells themselves.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:46.000
That's what I do.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:52.400
And when you look at that data you
realize that, with those expensive

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:58.720
installation costs, what people who make
panels need to do is raise the efficiency.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:05.200
Because then you can get the power you
need with a lower number of panels.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:17.030
And you can calculate how valuable it
is to make a panel more efficient.

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:20.690
And if you run those
numbers you find that you

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:27.610
should be willing to pay an extra three
cents per watt for an efficiency point.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:33.720
So in other words If you could get
a 20% efficient panel instead of 19,

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:39.560
you should be willing to pay $0.53 per
watt for it instead of $0.50 per watt.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:43.259
And indeed, if you look at the market,

00:04:43.259 --> 00:04:47.971
there's a range of products
where the efficiency

00:04:47.971 --> 00:04:52.358
goes from 15% efficiency up to about 21.

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:56.720
And indeed,
those are the variations in the price.

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:00.560
In some cases,
people are willing to pay even more.

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:07.642
For example, in the neighborhood where
I live in, in Menlo Park, California.

00:05:07.642 --> 00:05:12.200
A lot of people have to pay up
to 40 cent per watt, I'm sorry,

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:17.533
40 cent per kilowatt hour for
their electricity because we have a tiered

00:05:17.533 --> 00:05:22.367
system and the more power you use,
the higher rate you have to pay.

00:05:22.367 --> 00:05:26.141
And a lot of people are switching
to electric vehicles and

00:05:26.141 --> 00:05:29.288
that's raising their electricity needs and
so

00:05:29.288 --> 00:05:34.980
they might want to install six or
seven kilowatts of panels on their roof.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:40.400
But they also don't have very large roofs
and a lot of their roof is not useful

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:45.010
because they have chimneys and vents and
some of the roof might be shaded.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:48.090
And some of the roofs have complex shapes.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:53.110
And so they simply can't get
the capacity that they would like

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:57.240
when the panels only have 15 or
16% efficiency.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:02.130
And so it can make sense for
them to really pay a premium

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:05.020
to get higher efficiency panels.

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:08.700
And so
I think there's a strong drive for that.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:15.840
And here on the next slide we
can see how the efficiency

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.810
has evolved over time for
some of the most important technologies.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:24.300
Let's start with that black
curve because that's silicon.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.000
And right now about 90,

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:32.310
maybe 92% of the market is crystal and
silicone solar cells.

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:36.620
And the world record efficiency is 25% but
that's for

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:42.400
a small cell and not for
expensive things were done to make it.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:48.621
The panels would be more
at maybe 15 to 21%.

00:06:48.621 --> 00:06:52.297
But the other thing I want you
to see from that curve, is there

00:06:52.297 --> 00:06:57.128
is hardly been any progress in raising
that efficiency since the late 90's so

00:06:57.128 --> 00:06:59.740
that efficiency is pretty much stagnant.

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:04.931
Above it, that's the green curve,
that's gallium arsenide and

00:07:04.931 --> 00:07:07.033
you can go up to 28% with that.

00:07:07.033 --> 00:07:09.348
But it's a very expensive
technology right now.

00:07:09.348 --> 00:07:13.601
It's about 40 times more
expensive than silicon.

00:07:13.601 --> 00:07:18.910
And I don't see a pathway for
a dramatic cost reduction there.

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:24.810
If you look at the pink and blue those
are so called thin film technologies.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:28.330
Copper, indium, gallium selenide and
cadmium telluride.

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:33.527
And the idea there is to use a smaller
amount of semiconductor to try to

00:07:33.527 --> 00:07:38.545
reduce costs, and you can see
the efficiency evolved over time and

00:07:38.545 --> 00:07:40.887
now both of them are at 22%.

00:07:40.887 --> 00:07:46.671
But, the news of today is that
red curve the perovskite,

00:07:46.671 --> 00:07:49.573
it just came out of nowhere.

00:07:49.573 --> 00:07:54.722
Around 2012 and
the efficiency has soared up to 22,

00:07:54.722 --> 00:07:58.829
catching up with CIGS and
Cadmium telluride.

00:07:58.829 --> 00:08:03.929
And never in history has
a semiconductor advanced so quickly for

00:08:03.929 --> 00:08:09.326
solar salt applications and
I think they're two reasons for that.

00:08:09.326 --> 00:08:13.282
One, there are some very beneficial
properties that I'm getting ready to tell

00:08:13.282 --> 00:08:13.884
you about.

00:08:13.884 --> 00:08:19.564
And the other is that there's a community
of solar cell researchers who've learned

00:08:19.564 --> 00:08:24.684
a lot over the years and these people
really know how to take a new material and

00:08:24.684 --> 00:08:28.613
make a good solar cell with it,
so things move faster now.

00:08:32.609 --> 00:08:38.003
Maybe there are some of you out there who
don't have an engineering background,

00:08:38.003 --> 00:08:41.587
so let me say just a little
bit about semiconductors.

00:08:41.587 --> 00:08:46.470
These are materials that have a bunch
of continuous energy levels that

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:51.351
are filled with electrons that make
up what we call the valence band and

00:08:51.351 --> 00:08:53.336
then on the energy diagram,

00:08:53.336 --> 00:08:58.901
there are a lot of energies where there
are no states that electrons can go into.

00:08:58.901 --> 00:09:03.962
And then there's a mostly empty
band called the conduction band and

00:09:03.962 --> 00:09:08.489
in between that is something
called the energy band gap, and

00:09:08.489 --> 00:09:12.150
I will refer to that number
a lot in this webinar.

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:18.190
When light comes in,
we can excite an electron from

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:22.840
the valence band up to
the conduction band and

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:28.990
it will fall to the bottom of band if
we excite it up high into the band.

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:33.946
And in fall that electron
falls back down in a process,

00:09:33.946 --> 00:09:38.163
we call recombination
that energy is stored.

00:09:38.163 --> 00:09:43.462
And what we want to do is get the electron
to go to one side of the semiconductor and

00:09:43.462 --> 00:09:47.636
get the hole or the positive charge
to go to the other side, and

00:09:47.636 --> 00:09:50.760
we want to extract that
power as electricity.

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:53.866
That's basically how a solar cell works.

00:09:56.606 --> 00:09:58.904
And the band ap is really important.

00:09:58.904 --> 00:10:04.330
Because if we have a large band gap,
then most of the photons in the solar

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:10.766
spectrum don't have enough energy to get
the electron up into the conduction band.

00:10:10.766 --> 00:10:14.280
And so, we can't absorb those photons.

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:20.830
On the other hand, if we pick a small band
gap, then the photons excite the carriers

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:25.800
high up into the band and
then they fall to the bottom of the band

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:30.640
in a process we call thermalization and
we lose all that energy.

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:34.480
And so with a low band gap,
we get a low voltage.

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:38.620
With a high band gap, we get a high
voltage, but we get a low current,

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:43.220
because we get one charge
carrier per photon absorbed.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:46.819
And so on the curve of
efficiency versus band gap,

00:10:46.819 --> 00:10:51.202
you see that it starts low,
rises to a peak and then it falls.

00:10:51.202 --> 00:10:55.288
And from this we see that
the ideal band gap is about

00:10:55.288 --> 00:10:59.473
1.4 electron volts and
with 1 semiconductor,

00:10:59.473 --> 00:11:04.743
the highest efficiency you can
get with the Sun is about 31%.

00:11:10.662 --> 00:11:12.023
Now, there is a trick.

00:11:12.023 --> 00:11:14.804
There's several tricks for
beating that limit, but

00:11:14.804 --> 00:11:18.520
I'm going to focus on what I
think is the most important one.

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:20.280
We can make tandems or

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:25.420
other people call them multijunction
solar cells and the idea

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:30.170
is to put a large band gap solar cell
on top of a small band gap solar cell.

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:36.290
When you do that, the high energy photons
are absorbed in the large and gap cell.

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:40.723
And because it has a large band gap,
we can generate a higher voltage.

00:11:40.723 --> 00:11:44.680
So we can capture more of
the energy from those photons and

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:48.390
then the low energy photons,
they will go through and

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:53.185
we'll use a second cell with a smaller
band out to harvest those two.

00:11:53.185 --> 00:11:55.012
So, we can do better that way.

00:11:55.012 --> 00:11:59.152
And theoretically, we can do up to 42%.

00:11:59.152 --> 00:12:05.584
But practically for one junction,
silicon is limited to about 25.

00:12:05.584 --> 00:12:07.875
And I think practically,

00:12:07.875 --> 00:12:13.914
35% might be about the best we
can do with cost-effective means,

00:12:13.914 --> 00:12:18.923
but 35% is considerably
more efficient than 25%.

00:12:18.923 --> 00:12:21.510
And so, I think it's an attractive goal.

00:12:25.777 --> 00:12:29.809
This plot here just shows
the efficiency you can get,

00:12:29.809 --> 00:12:33.223
as a function of the band
gaps on the y-axis.

00:12:33.223 --> 00:12:36.811
That's the band gap of
the low band gap cell.

00:12:36.811 --> 00:12:38.140
The x-axis is the high.

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:43.252
You can see there that 42 is possible and
it tells us that for

00:12:43.252 --> 00:12:50.484
this application, the band gaps we want
are about 1.1 electron volts and 1.8.

00:12:50.484 --> 00:12:55.987
So some numbers to keep in mind as
I go forward for single junction,

00:12:55.987 --> 00:13:01.509
we want 1.4 eV and for
2 junctions, we want 1.1 and 1.8.

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:06.690
Now let me tell you about
the perovskites themselves.

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:15.140
Perovskites are essentially a salt and
you can dissolve them in a polar solvent.

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:19.590
And when you do, you really don't have
perovskite in your vial of solution,

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:21.199
you have ions in there.

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:25.709
But then when you cast it or print it and

00:13:25.709 --> 00:13:30.450
form a thin film,
the solvent will evaporate away.

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:34.706
And as it does, this salt will form and
it will be solid and

00:13:34.706 --> 00:13:40.571
you will get a crystal structure like you
can see on the right side of the slide.

00:13:40.571 --> 00:13:45.390
The word perovskite, what it really
means is just that crystal structure.

00:13:45.390 --> 00:13:49.814
It means the atoms
are arranged in that way, but

00:13:49.814 --> 00:13:55.121
a specific example of perovskites
that makes a good solar

00:13:55.121 --> 00:14:01.440
cell is methylammonium lead iodide,
but there are other options.

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:08.211
And on the next slide, you'll see on the
top as we go from the left to the right,

00:14:08.211 --> 00:14:12.625
we're gradually reducing
the amount of iodine and

00:14:12.625 --> 00:14:17.962
adding in bromine and
you can see that the color is changing and

00:14:17.962 --> 00:14:22.068
that's because the band
gap is increasing from

00:14:22.068 --> 00:14:26.410
1.6 electron volts to 2.3 electron volts.

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:30.851
The visible spectrum is
from about 2 eV up to 3 eV.

00:14:30.851 --> 00:14:34.932
So when the band gap is 1.6,
the semiconductor can absorb

00:14:34.932 --> 00:14:39.710
all of the visible photons and
that makes the film look black.

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:45.533
But as we go to the right, we get to
where the film cannot absorb the red and

00:14:45.533 --> 00:14:51.852
then it can't absorb the yellow and
that's why the film starts to be colored.

00:14:51.852 --> 00:14:57.019
If you look at the bottom,
we've replaced the methylammonium

00:14:57.019 --> 00:15:02.674
with another organic cation called
formamidinium and you see that,

00:15:02.674 --> 00:15:07.270
that can drop the band gap
from 1.6 down to 1.48.

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:12.749
And later in the talk, I'll show you
can we can replace lead with tin.

00:15:12.749 --> 00:15:17.884
So by changing all of
the elements in the formula ABF3,

00:15:17.884 --> 00:15:25.960
we have a variety of knobs for adjusting
the band gap for whatever application.

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:28.493
By the way, if you use chlorine,

00:15:28.493 --> 00:15:33.918
you can raise the band gap all the way
up to 3 eV and you can also make red,

00:15:33.918 --> 00:15:38.562
green and blue LEDs, but
that would be a top for another day.

00:15:41.531 --> 00:15:42.924
On this slide,

00:15:42.924 --> 00:15:48.830
you can see the cross section of
a typical perovskite solar cell.

00:15:48.830 --> 00:15:57.041
On the left was the original
composition that a few labs.

00:15:57.041 --> 00:16:03.380
Like Namgu Park and Tom Niasaka and Henry
Snaith and Michael Gratzfol discovered.

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:07.420
And it evolved from the dye
sensitized solar cell.

00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:13.769
It has a film of titanium nanoparticles
filled in with the perovskite and

00:16:13.769 --> 00:16:17.724
then sandwiched between
a gold electrode and

00:16:17.724 --> 00:16:22.525
a transparent fluorine-doped
tin oxide electrode.

00:16:22.525 --> 00:16:26.935
But pretty quickly Henry Snaith
realized that you don't actually need

00:16:26.935 --> 00:16:28.856
the titanium nanoparticles.

00:16:28.856 --> 00:16:32.096
And you can use the plainer
structure on the right,

00:16:32.096 --> 00:16:35.650
where it's just an uninterrupted
film of perovskite.

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:43.500
On the bottom is an energy diagram and one
of the key design rules is shown there.

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:45.630
On the left is Titania and

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:49.820
that's an example of what we call
an electron transport material and

00:16:49.820 --> 00:16:55.520
it needs to have its conduction band which
is that top energy level being lower

00:16:55.520 --> 00:17:00.640
than the conduction band of the perovskite
that way electrons can go over there.

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:05.220
But holes, which go upward on
an energy diagram, they can't go in.

00:17:05.220 --> 00:17:09.190
And that way electrons can
only escape to the left.

00:17:09.190 --> 00:17:12.369
In contrast, on the right side,
we have a material,

00:17:12.369 --> 00:17:15.773
just generically called
the whole transport material.

00:17:15.773 --> 00:17:20.489
It can accept holes, but
it cannot except electrons.

00:17:20.489 --> 00:17:26.050
So these materials, sometimes we call them
selective or carrier selective contacts,

00:17:26.050 --> 00:17:30.405
and they provide the mechanism to
have electrons go out on one side and

00:17:30.405 --> 00:17:31.700
holes on the other.

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:39.900
Perovskites have many
wonderful properties.

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:42.690
We've seen they have a tunable band gap.

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:48.679
They're very highly absorbing, they absorb
as well as any semiconductor out there.

00:17:48.679 --> 00:17:51.690
If you illuminate them
with pulses of light,

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:55.350
you can see that the charge
carriers live for a long time.

00:17:55.350 --> 00:17:58.240
That means there's very
slow recombination,

00:17:58.240 --> 00:18:02.079
which is one of the most desirable
properties for a solar cell.

00:18:02.079 --> 00:18:05.954
A lot of semiconductors have
defects at their surfaces and

00:18:05.954 --> 00:18:11.343
charge carriers will recombine there,
but that is very slow in the Pervoskite.

00:18:11.343 --> 00:18:16.308
For a lot of semiconductors, if you have
a thin film with lots of small crystals,

00:18:16.308 --> 00:18:20.410
there will be recombination at
the boundaries between crystals.

00:18:20.410 --> 00:18:22.833
But that seems to be very
slow with perovskites.

00:18:22.833 --> 00:18:27.022
And it's all of those
properties that make it

00:18:27.022 --> 00:18:31.329
possible to get as high as 22% efficiency.

00:18:31.329 --> 00:18:35.426
And moreover,
we can print these things even on plastic.

00:18:35.426 --> 00:18:40.600
And so we can, we can make a flexible
solar cell at low temperature.

00:18:40.600 --> 00:18:45.094
And that's the property that cadmium
telluride and sigs don't have.

00:18:45.094 --> 00:18:50.556
So that's the good news.

00:18:50.556 --> 00:18:55.780
A balanced webinar has to present
some of the bad news as well.

00:18:55.780 --> 00:18:58.319
These materials are very
sensitive to water.

00:18:58.319 --> 00:19:01.907
A lot of you probably know
that faults take up water and

00:19:01.907 --> 00:19:04.471
these perovskites are no exception.

00:19:04.471 --> 00:19:09.841
But that just really means that
the panels need to be packaged well and

00:19:09.841 --> 00:19:15.684
it means that you need to cover
the perovskite with an electrode promptly.

00:19:15.684 --> 00:19:19.826
In the factory, you wouldn't want to
have perovskite film sitting around,

00:19:19.826 --> 00:19:22.880
especially if the factory
were in a humid environment.

00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:26.780
So you need to get it packaged promptly.

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:33.270
Another one, it turns out the halogens,
that's the iodine or the bromine,

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:41.270
they move around very easily and they
can react with metal, especially silver.

00:19:41.270 --> 00:19:45.970
But I'll show you in a slide or
two how to avoid that problem.

00:19:45.970 --> 00:19:51.010
And finally, methylammonium can
leave the film relatively easy.

00:19:51.010 --> 00:19:55.860
It'll slowly come out at 80 degrees and
by the time you're at 100,

00:19:55.860 --> 00:20:00.260
110 degrees,
it will come out very quickly.

00:20:00.260 --> 00:20:04.370
And that could be a problem for
field use, where the temperature,

00:20:04.370 --> 00:20:10.430
you might hit 80 degrees C, but it's also
a problem in manufacturing where sometimes

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:14.910
you might have a processing step at,
say, 130 degrees.

00:20:18.998 --> 00:20:23.680
My group has figured out how to
mitigate some of these problems.

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:28.390
We wanted to put a transparent
indium tin oxide electrode on top.

00:20:28.390 --> 00:20:33.980
That's the material that's used
in your flat panel display and

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:38.850
your laptop and
we wanted to sputter it but it turned out

00:20:38.850 --> 00:20:44.340
that process will damage the perovskite so
we needed to put down a buffer layer.

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:49.030
And for that, we used aluminum
doped zinc oxide nanoparticles.

00:20:49.030 --> 00:20:54.300
We could deposit those from solution in
a way that did not damage the perovskite.

00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:57.080
And then, we could sputter on that.

00:20:57.080 --> 00:20:58.800
These are inorganic nanoparticles.

00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:03.400
They're a little more robust and
they would protect the perovskite.

00:21:04.470 --> 00:21:06.256
And when we did that,

00:21:06.256 --> 00:21:12.380
we would put these solar cells on
a hot plate in air at 100 degrees.

00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:14.920
And if you look at the picture here,

00:21:14.920 --> 00:21:18.950
you'll see the square in the center,
that's where the ITO is.

00:21:18.950 --> 00:21:22.590
And in there,
the perovskite maintained its color.

00:21:22.590 --> 00:21:28.240
But outside of that, where there was no
indium tin oxide, we found the material

00:21:28.240 --> 00:21:36.250
very quickly converted to lead iodide
as the methyl ammonium left the film.

00:21:36.250 --> 00:21:41.479
So this demonstrated that the ITO
would hold the methylammonium in and

00:21:41.479 --> 00:21:47.606
moreover, we found that iodine doesn't
seem to react with the indium tin oxide.

00:21:47.606 --> 00:21:52.558
And so we could run this solar cell
in there with no packaging for

00:21:52.558 --> 00:21:57.986
a week, on a hot plate at 100
degrees under one sun illumination.

00:21:57.986 --> 00:22:00.588
So this is a very big breakthrough,

00:22:00.588 --> 00:22:05.640
switching away from metals to using
the indium tin oxide electrode.

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:10.520
But that's only one of
the things you can do.

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:14.910
Before I give the next, let me just also
point out some other research groups have

00:22:14.910 --> 00:22:19.020
found you can also use carbon
paste as an electrode.

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:24.540
Again, showing that the key to stability
seems to be getting away from metal.

00:22:24.540 --> 00:22:30.320
And the second paper explains in
detail why gold is problematic.

00:22:30.320 --> 00:22:33.060
It actually diffuses into the perovskite.

00:22:35.470 --> 00:22:40.560
So the second thing we do to keep
methylammonium from escaping is simply not

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:46.500
use methylammonium and if you use
mixtures of cesium and formamidinium,

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:53.980
you get a compound that we find to
be stable at 150 degrees Celsius.

00:22:53.980 --> 00:23:01.180
Cesium is a little bit too small to
properly fit into the perovskite lattice.

00:23:01.180 --> 00:23:03.980
Formamidinium is a little bit too large.

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:11.010
But mixtures of the two seem to make a
very robust perovskite crystal structure.

00:23:13.610 --> 00:23:16.710
And then the third really
important strategy is

00:23:16.710 --> 00:23:20.280
simply to properly
package the solar cells.

00:23:20.280 --> 00:23:23.310
And we talked a lot to the team

00:23:23.310 --> 00:23:28.660
at DuPont here at Silicon Valley who
makes a lot of these packaging materials.

00:23:28.660 --> 00:23:34.116
And then we went to a company called
D2 Solar that packages solar cells and

00:23:34.116 --> 00:23:40.278
makes panels for other solar cell startups
that don't have all of that equipment.

00:23:40.278 --> 00:23:47.254
And here you put a rubber edge seal that
you can see in black around the edge and

00:23:47.254 --> 00:23:52.813
then in the interior,
you have encapsulation made of things

00:23:52.813 --> 00:23:58.166
like ethylene vinyl acetate or
an ionomer called cerlen.

00:23:58.166 --> 00:24:03.172
And the you pull vacuum to get any
air out and you heat it to about

00:24:03.172 --> 00:24:08.290
130 degrees and press on it and
forms a really nice package.

00:24:08.290 --> 00:24:13.749
And this is just industry standard
packaging for solar cells.

00:24:13.749 --> 00:24:19.509
And then when you do this, you can put
it through a torture test that the solar

00:24:19.509 --> 00:24:25.280
industry uses to predict if something
is going to last outside for 25 years.

00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:30.023
They test for
1,000 hours at 85 degrees Celsius and

00:24:30.023 --> 00:24:35.050
85% humidity,
which is a really aggressive test.

00:24:35.050 --> 00:24:40.110
And we did that and
one of our three packages failed.

00:24:40.110 --> 00:24:42.710
It leaked and water got in.

00:24:42.710 --> 00:24:49.280
But two of them didn't and
we passed this test after 1,000 hours.

00:24:49.280 --> 00:24:52.661
Actually, the efficiency increased, and so

00:24:52.661 --> 00:24:56.830
this is extremely encouraging
that we passed that test.

00:24:59.380 --> 00:25:05.240
So the outlook on stability is that if
you use the more stable Perovskites,

00:25:05.240 --> 00:25:08.860
you use impermeable and
unreactive electrodes and

00:25:08.860 --> 00:25:14.780
proper packaging, it looks like at least
thermal stability can be obtained.

00:25:14.780 --> 00:25:20.211
We've also done a temperature cycling
torture test where we go back and

00:25:20.211 --> 00:25:25.912
forth from 80 degrees C into a freezer
at -40 to see if the shrinking and

00:25:25.912 --> 00:25:28.824
expansion will cause it to pop apart.

00:25:28.824 --> 00:25:31.740
And we passed that on the first time.

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:37.580
And we're working on long-term tests
under light but those take a bit longer.

00:25:37.580 --> 00:25:41.160
And at least after 1,000 hours,
things look encouraging.

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:46.120
But it'll be a while before
we'll have longer term tests.

00:25:46.120 --> 00:25:48.090
So a year ago,

00:25:48.090 --> 00:25:53.920
I was a bit scared over the prospects

00:25:53.920 --> 00:25:57.940
of making these devices stable because
they were only lasting for minutes.

00:25:57.940 --> 00:26:00.250
But huge progress has been made and

00:26:00.250 --> 00:26:03.171
I think the outlook is
positive now on stability.

00:26:06.450 --> 00:26:10.670
Now let me turn back to
the Tandem Solar Cell.

00:26:10.670 --> 00:26:12.810
So we know this concept works.

00:26:12.810 --> 00:26:20.070
The world record is 46% and that's made
with single crystals 3/5 semi conductors.

00:26:20.070 --> 00:26:22.820
Basically the Gallium Arsenite family.

00:26:22.820 --> 00:26:26.860
I think that world record has
four different band gaps in it.

00:26:26.860 --> 00:26:31.692
Unfortunately, those cells are absolutely
tiny because they cost over

00:26:31.692 --> 00:26:35.520
$40,000 per meter squared to make.

00:26:35.520 --> 00:26:41.750
So I believe the future is a much,
much cheaper tandem.

00:26:41.750 --> 00:26:47.510
And on the bottom, I think we could just
use conventional semiconductors like

00:26:47.510 --> 00:26:54.370
silicon or copper indium gallium selenide,
which both have the perfect 1.1V band gap.

00:26:54.370 --> 00:26:58.610
And then on the top, we need something
that works even when it's not a single

00:26:58.610 --> 00:27:02.470
crystal, something that you
would say is defect tolerant.

00:27:02.470 --> 00:27:08.917
And the Perovskites have really merged
as an exciting candidate for that.

00:27:11.316 --> 00:27:16.246
There are a couple of ways that people
could imagine making these tandems or

00:27:16.246 --> 00:27:19.810
actually probably far more than two but
here.

00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:22.730
in my view, the two most promising.

00:27:22.730 --> 00:27:27.960
You could make a complete Perovskite
cell that has it's own electrodes,

00:27:27.960 --> 00:27:31.500
and mechanically stack it on top of,

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:35.100
let's just say a silicon cell
that has it's own electrodes.

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:40.990
An advantage of that is that You can just
use any silicon cell, you could get them

00:27:40.990 --> 00:27:46.310
from different companies and they wouldn't
have to modify their product at all.

00:27:46.310 --> 00:27:50.510
And you just package
the Perovskite cells on top.

00:27:51.890 --> 00:27:56.830
On the other hand, you can make
a monolithic cell where the Perovskite

00:27:56.830 --> 00:28:01.550
is directly deposited onto the silicon or
CIGS solar cell.

00:28:01.550 --> 00:28:06.040
If you do that,
you have to match the current densities.

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:09.558
If one of them puts out less
current than the other,

00:28:09.558 --> 00:28:13.460
it will set the current for the stack, and

00:28:13.460 --> 00:28:19.200
that means you have to calculate and
obtain exactly the right band gaps so

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:25.100
that the two cells each get
the right amount of current.

00:28:25.100 --> 00:28:30.640
And there's some engineering that has
to be done to make the right interfaces

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:33.100
between the two.

00:28:33.100 --> 00:28:39.230
With the mechanically stacked tandem,
you don't have to perfectly current match.

00:28:39.230 --> 00:28:44.010
On the next slide I'll show, you can still
wire them up in a series of each other.

00:28:44.010 --> 00:28:48.930
You can have a string of silicon and
a series of a string of Perovskite cells.

00:28:48.930 --> 00:28:54.690
And you can just simply adjust the areas
in order to get current matching.

00:28:54.690 --> 00:29:03.391
And if you do that, your panel will still
only have two wires coming out of it.

00:29:03.391 --> 00:29:08.401
A few years ago, my group set out
to demonstrate that all of this was

00:29:08.401 --> 00:29:14.486
possible and that the easier one to make
is the mechanically-stacked tandem and

00:29:14.486 --> 00:29:20.931
that's what we did, and back then our
Perovskite only had 12.3% efficiency and

00:29:20.931 --> 00:29:27.225
we put it on top of 17% efficient silicone
and we also had 17% efficient CIGS.

00:29:27.225 --> 00:29:32.153
And when you put those under the
Perovskite, the efficiency of course drops

00:29:32.153 --> 00:29:36.420
because a lot of the photons
are absorbed in the top cell.

00:29:36.420 --> 00:29:40.650
But you could still get 5.7 and
if you add those up,

00:29:40.650 --> 00:29:44.990
you'd get 18, which is better
than either cell by itself.

00:29:46.338 --> 00:29:48.580
Since then,
there've been a lot of advances and

00:29:48.580 --> 00:29:52.990
I'll just have to skip straight to
the world record and this is from

00:29:52.990 --> 00:29:58.460
Christophe Ballif's lab in Switzerland and
with a lot of things optimized.

00:29:58.460 --> 00:30:02.230
The efficiency is already at 25%.

00:30:02.230 --> 00:30:09.921
And I see a clear path to get
this up to 30% efficiency.

00:30:09.921 --> 00:30:13.821
My group was also the first
to demonstrate a two

00:30:13.821 --> 00:30:18.726
terminal tandem in
partnership with Tony at MIT.

00:30:18.726 --> 00:30:22.146
They made a silicon saw with
a tunnel junction on top.

00:30:22.146 --> 00:30:25.575
And then we put a Perovskite
saw on top of it.

00:30:25.575 --> 00:30:30.776
And here we got 13.7% and
we identified we were having a big

00:30:30.776 --> 00:30:35.783
problem with our whole transport
layer absorbing photons,

00:30:35.783 --> 00:30:38.450
which we didn't want to it to do.

00:30:38.450 --> 00:30:42.763
But a lot of progress has
been made on that as well.

00:30:42.763 --> 00:30:48.823
Now the worldwide community has made
many compositions of Perovskites and

00:30:48.823 --> 00:30:51.140
we have a lot to choose from.

00:30:52.430 --> 00:30:56.060
We don't have a great 1.0
eV band gap material but

00:30:56.060 --> 00:31:00.290
we do have some pretty good
ones with 1.6 electron volts.

00:31:00.290 --> 00:31:05.435
And in this next slide you'll see,
with 17% Thesium,

00:31:05.435 --> 00:31:10.475
83% Phormidium and
17% Bromine, 83% Iodine,

00:31:10.475 --> 00:31:17.760
we've been able to make a 2-terminal
tandem with 23.6% efficiency.

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:23.350
That was a validated world record by NREL,
about ten days ago.

00:31:24.780 --> 00:31:29.050
And this is also,
this is the same composition

00:31:29.050 --> 00:31:32.520
that passed those damp heat tests
that I talked about earlier.

00:31:32.520 --> 00:31:37.260
So when we selected this composition,
it was not only for

00:31:37.260 --> 00:31:42.218
efficiency but we were also
selecting it to be very stable.

00:31:47.571 --> 00:31:52.304
Finally, I want to tell you about
something that's not published yet

00:31:52.304 --> 00:31:54.270
in a peer reviewed journal.

00:31:54.270 --> 00:32:00.173
&gt;&gt; In collaboration with Henry Snaith's
group from Oxford, we've figured

00:32:00.173 --> 00:32:05.711
out to make a low band gap perovskite and
make all perovskite tandems.

00:32:05.711 --> 00:32:13.326
This is on the Archives Journal right now,
as the paper is going through peer review.

00:32:13.326 --> 00:32:21.678
And here, we're adjusting the ratio
of lead to tin in the compound.

00:32:21.678 --> 00:32:25.849
And if the tin composition is around 50%,

00:32:25.849 --> 00:32:30.804
we can get the band gap
down to 1.2 electron volts.

00:32:30.804 --> 00:32:35.146
And that's not perfect for
a two-terminal tandem, but

00:32:35.146 --> 00:32:39.411
it's getting very close to
being the perfect band gap.

00:32:39.411 --> 00:32:45.446
And here, you see the cross-sectional
SCM of a two-terminal tandem.

00:32:45.446 --> 00:32:49.923
And right now,
we're at about 17% efficiency and

00:32:49.923 --> 00:32:53.714
we understand what some
of the problems are.

00:32:53.714 --> 00:32:56.989
Our 1.2 eV material is just far too thin.

00:32:56.989 --> 00:33:01.828
We just haven't figured out how to deposit
a thick, high quality film yet, but

00:33:01.828 --> 00:33:04.515
I think that will
certainly be figured out.

00:33:04.515 --> 00:33:09.872
And we needed a 1.8 eV band gap
to have proper current matching,

00:33:09.872 --> 00:33:14.464
but we also need a better composition for
that band gap and

00:33:14.464 --> 00:33:18.693
it's a fundamental reason
why that can't be done.

00:33:18.693 --> 00:33:23.418
So, I expect the sufficiency
to rise very quickly.

00:33:23.418 --> 00:33:27.589
We also made a four-terminal tandem
with the low band gap material.

00:33:27.589 --> 00:33:32.548
And right now, we're at about 20%
efficiency and it's a very new material.

00:33:32.548 --> 00:33:36.895
We haven't been able to do
full stability testing, but

00:33:36.895 --> 00:33:41.991
it was completely stable over
20 hours of testing under 1 sum.

00:33:41.991 --> 00:33:47.238
So, I think the outlook for
efficiency and stability is wonderful.

00:33:47.238 --> 00:33:52.111
You've seen how the single junction
efficiency rapidly rose to 22,

00:33:52.111 --> 00:33:55.531
I'd be very surprised if
it's saturated there.

00:33:55.531 --> 00:34:01.847
I'm optimistic that single
junction cells might go to 25 and

00:34:01.847 --> 00:34:07.101
that the tandems will go
up over 30% efficiency.

00:34:07.101 --> 00:34:12.568
I know someone's going to ask me about
the implications of lead being toxic,

00:34:12.568 --> 00:34:16.666
so let me address that before
we turn it over to the Q&amp;A.

00:34:16.666 --> 00:34:21.766
There's this paper in
Nature Materials that's written by

00:34:21.766 --> 00:34:28.704
toxicologists who have no reason to be
biased towards perovskite solar cells and

00:34:28.704 --> 00:34:34.026
they talk about what happens
when people are exposed to lead.

00:34:34.026 --> 00:34:38.969
And one thing that is very clear is
that factories would have to be run

00:34:38.969 --> 00:34:43.914
in a proper way to protect the workers and
make sure that they're not

00:34:43.914 --> 00:34:48.860
exposed to lead, and there are plenty
of industries that use lead,

00:34:48.860 --> 00:34:53.849
and other toxic compounds, and
it is possible to protect workers.

00:34:53.849 --> 00:34:58.781
And then when the panels expire and
are decommissioned,

00:34:58.781 --> 00:35:03.197
it will be very important
to take the panel apart and

00:35:03.197 --> 00:35:08.968
properly capture the lead and
then something else to think about.

00:35:08.968 --> 00:35:14.626
These panels are only going to have
about one gram of lead in the perovskite

00:35:14.626 --> 00:35:20.584
layer and just to put that into
perspective, people may not realize this.

00:35:20.584 --> 00:35:24.984
But today,
panels already have 16 grams of lead.

00:35:24.984 --> 00:35:26.939
That is in the red solder.

00:35:26.939 --> 00:35:30.076
So, it's a very small amount of lead.

00:35:30.076 --> 00:35:33.988
And moreover, if you took the panel and
dashed on it for

00:35:33.988 --> 00:35:38.595
five minutes of a thread shimmer and
throw it into a lake.

00:35:38.595 --> 00:35:41.306
Well then, yes, the lead would escape.

00:35:41.306 --> 00:35:46.031
But short of doing things like that,
it would be very hard for

00:35:46.031 --> 00:35:50.490
the lead to actually get out
of a properly packaged module

00:35:54.047 --> 00:35:59.402
There are five companies that I
know of in the startup stage.

00:35:59.402 --> 00:36:04.652
Oxford PV is certainly the largest and
they're developing

00:36:04.652 --> 00:36:09.169
a two terminal tandem with
perovskite on silicon.

00:36:09.169 --> 00:36:13.189
Full disclosure, I have co-founded
Iris PV with Colin Bailey and

00:36:13.189 --> 00:36:15.502
we're taking a different approach.

00:36:15.502 --> 00:36:20.505
We are mechanically stacking
perovskites on silicon.

00:36:20.505 --> 00:36:24.736
Hunt Energy,
led by Michael Irwin in Dallas.

00:36:24.736 --> 00:36:30.193
They're going with single junction
perovskites, so they're going for

00:36:30.193 --> 00:36:35.751
an ultra-low cost product and Saule
in Poland seems to be doing flexible.

00:36:35.751 --> 00:36:40.799
Perovskite sales and
I don't know a lot about Weihua in China,

00:36:40.799 --> 00:36:45.373
but I have seen pictures of
panels that are almost two feet

00:36:45.373 --> 00:36:49.393
across that they've already
been able to print.

00:36:51.879 --> 00:36:53.622
If you want to learn more,

00:36:53.622 --> 00:36:59.184
we have a certificate program on energy
innovations and emerging technology.

00:36:59.184 --> 00:37:02.205
I teach a three-hour
module on solar cells.

00:37:02.205 --> 00:37:06.883
In that module, I say a whole lot more
about the industry in general and

00:37:06.883 --> 00:37:10.860
I go through all of the important
solar cell technologies.

00:37:10.860 --> 00:37:13.187
They're not just perovskites.

00:37:13.187 --> 00:37:15.764
And more broadly in this certificate,

00:37:15.764 --> 00:37:19.365
I think we cover all of
the important topics on energy.

00:37:19.365 --> 00:37:21.450
So, you can learn a whole lot there.

00:37:23.900 --> 00:37:27.180
These are all the people that are not all,

00:37:27.180 --> 00:37:31.990
but some of the people who
helped do the research

00:37:31.990 --> 00:37:37.050
that I talked about from Stanford and some
of our partners from outside Stanford and

00:37:37.050 --> 00:37:40.000
certainly none of this would
have been possible without them.

00:37:41.230 --> 00:37:45.079
With that, I thank you all for
attending and

00:37:45.079 --> 00:37:48.834
I will do my best to
answer your questions.

00:37:48.834 --> 00:37:49.388
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:37:49.388 --> 00:37:51.871
Thank you for
that excellent presentation and for

00:37:51.871 --> 00:37:54.957
sharing some of the very latest
research in collaborations.

00:37:54.957 --> 00:37:57.240
So now,
I'd like to hand it back over to Mike.

00:37:57.240 --> 00:37:59.760
So, he can answer some of the questions
that you've been sending through.

00:38:01.280 --> 00:38:04.110
&gt;&gt; They've typed a lot
of the questions for me,

00:38:04.110 --> 00:38:07.710
I can see them on the screen and
lots of good ones here.

00:38:07.710 --> 00:38:11.520
I'll start with,
are perovskites flexible or rigid?

00:38:13.050 --> 00:38:16.780
The films we're using
are about a half micron thick.

00:38:16.780 --> 00:38:22.150
And actually, pretty much everything
is flexible when it is that thin.

00:38:22.150 --> 00:38:23.820
So, what determines whether or

00:38:23.820 --> 00:38:29.380
not the solar cell is flexible is simply
what's substrate it is deposited on.

00:38:29.380 --> 00:38:33.170
And because our processing
is at low temperature,

00:38:33.170 --> 00:38:39.130
we can build the solar cell on plastic,
which is flexible and

00:38:39.130 --> 00:38:45.220
there are indeed demonstrations in
the literature of flexible solar cells.

00:38:45.220 --> 00:38:50.218
I will say for applications though,
the plastic will need to

00:38:50.218 --> 00:38:54.433
keep the water out for
the lifetime of the product.

00:38:54.433 --> 00:38:58.850
And right now,
we don't have any expensive flexible

00:38:58.850 --> 00:39:02.493
packages that provide a suitable barrier.

00:39:02.493 --> 00:39:06.245
So, it going to be important for
there to be break through on that.

00:39:06.245 --> 00:39:11.028
But if the product didn't need to have
a long life time, then it would be

00:39:11.028 --> 00:39:16.230
certainly be possible right now to do
flexible perovskite case solar cells.

00:39:18.690 --> 00:39:24.041
Another question,
would it be possible to go to 100% tin for

00:39:24.041 --> 00:39:27.124
the purpose of getting the lead out?

00:39:27.124 --> 00:39:30.621
And people are working hard on that and

00:39:30.621 --> 00:39:35.325
I'm not sure what the latest
efficiency is there.

00:39:35.325 --> 00:39:40.121
I'm sure people have gotten 8% and

00:39:40.121 --> 00:39:45.222
I I think it's in that maybe 8 to 10,

00:39:45.222 --> 00:39:50.337
perhaps as high as 12% with all tin.

00:39:50.337 --> 00:39:53.110
So it's quite not yet, as good as led.

00:39:53.110 --> 00:39:59.740
However, there have been reports that
tin is actually more toxic than lead.

00:39:59.740 --> 00:40:05.970
The toxicity of lead is very well known,
but it actually,

00:40:05.970 --> 00:40:10.960
as far as toxic things go,
lead is not that bad and there are a lot

00:40:10.960 --> 00:40:15.690
of things that are worse that just
somehow haven't picked up the reputation.

00:40:15.690 --> 00:40:18.270
So I'm not sure switching to tin for

00:40:18.270 --> 00:40:24.660
the purpose of reducing toxicity
is going to be helpful.

00:40:24.660 --> 00:40:29.760
People are also switching to bismuth,
which unfortunately doesn't have

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:35.460
the right charge, or
it's not a plus two, it's a plus three.

00:40:35.460 --> 00:40:39.636
And then you have to balance that with
a plus one, so if you use silver and

00:40:39.636 --> 00:40:44.347
bismuth, for example,
Hema Karunadasa at Stanford does that, but

00:40:44.347 --> 00:40:49.180
it's not yet anywhere near as
good as these lead compounds.

00:40:49.180 --> 00:40:57.177
So suffice it to say, there are research
groups trying to find lead-free compounds.

00:41:00.798 --> 00:41:03.000
Looking at some of the other questions.

00:41:03.000 --> 00:41:09.210
People are asking, yeah,
what would the cost of these panels be?

00:41:09.210 --> 00:41:11.807
That's a great question.

00:41:11.807 --> 00:41:17.533
So we have worked with Mike Woodhouse
at the National Renewable Energy Lab,

00:41:17.533 --> 00:41:20.669
who leads a team that does cost modeling.

00:41:20.669 --> 00:41:25.435
And they call companies and they find out
what all the materials are going to cost.

00:41:25.435 --> 00:41:28.970
And they find out how
much it would cost to

00:41:28.970 --> 00:41:32.010
use the tools to do all
of the processing steps.

00:41:33.100 --> 00:41:37.412
And people in industry seem
to agree that the results

00:41:37.412 --> 00:41:40.553
from that group are pretty accurate.

00:41:40.553 --> 00:41:45.737
And so they project that if you just
did single junction perovskite panels,

00:41:45.737 --> 00:41:49.949
they'd be just a little bit
cheaper than cadmium telluride,

00:41:49.949 --> 00:41:53.610
because you can deposit
the perovskite more easily.

00:41:54.770 --> 00:41:59.590
But not dramatically cheaper and that's
because so much of the cost is in things

00:41:59.590 --> 00:42:04.710
like the packaging and the junction box,
where the wires come out.

00:42:04.710 --> 00:42:07.190
So that's why like when people tell you

00:42:07.190 --> 00:42:12.670
they're going to drop the cost by an order
of magnitude using solution processing,

00:42:12.670 --> 00:42:16.310
they usually have not accounted for
the packaging.

00:42:17.900 --> 00:42:21.170
In the tandems,
the way we like to think of it is,

00:42:21.170 --> 00:42:25.940
let's say you have a silicon panel and
you want to upgrade to a tandem.

00:42:27.020 --> 00:42:29.066
We project that down the road,

00:42:29.066 --> 00:42:33.030
the silicon panel would be
about $80 per meter squared.

00:42:33.030 --> 00:42:38.520
We think the upgrade cost is going to
be about $24 per meter squared.

00:42:38.520 --> 00:42:43.560
When you convert it to a dollar per watt,
we think that it'll be about the same.

00:42:43.560 --> 00:42:47.120
So it'll cost more, but
it'll also be more efficient.

00:42:47.120 --> 00:42:52.864
Where you really win is that with these
panels that have over 25% efficiency,

00:42:52.864 --> 00:42:57.940
you won't need to install as many of
them to get a given amount of power.

00:42:57.940 --> 00:43:03.874
And the big savings will be over on
the installation side of things and

00:43:03.874 --> 00:43:07.466
that's why it's compelling to do this.

00:43:10.899 --> 00:43:13.420
Okay, let me look at
some of these other ones.

00:43:13.420 --> 00:43:15.315
Someone's asking,

00:43:15.315 --> 00:43:21.370
what about the performance in
direct versus diffuse light?

00:43:22.590 --> 00:43:29.124
So these'll work fine in diffuse light,
unlike gallium arsenide cells,

00:43:29.124 --> 00:43:33.810
those are tiny and
they are used with concentrators.

00:43:33.810 --> 00:43:39.400
And those are either lenses or reflectors
that focus the light onto the cell,

00:43:39.400 --> 00:43:42.850
and they only work with the direct light.

00:43:42.850 --> 00:43:47.080
The diffuse light doesn't properly
get through the optical system, but

00:43:47.080 --> 00:43:51.470
we won't be using concentration and
so ours will work fine.

00:43:53.840 --> 00:43:59.602
There's a fine point
here with the tandems.

00:43:59.602 --> 00:44:02.704
Clouds change the spectrum and so

00:44:02.704 --> 00:44:07.909
if you have designed your tandem
to work well under the so

00:44:07.909 --> 00:44:14.221
called AM 1.5 conditions,
which is a bright day with no clouds,

00:44:14.221 --> 00:44:19.095
the tandem might not work
properly with clouds, nor

00:44:19.095 --> 00:44:25.690
will it work well at sunrise or
sunset when the spectrum has changed.

00:44:25.690 --> 00:44:27.500
It will still work.

00:44:27.500 --> 00:44:32.540
And my group and several others
are trying to calculate the loss

00:44:32.540 --> 00:44:37.762
over a typical year and it appears
that it could be around 10% to 15%.

00:44:37.762 --> 00:44:42.560
It's why I prefer
the mechanically stepped tandem,

00:44:42.560 --> 00:44:48.530
because we can use a little circuit
in-between the perovskite and

00:44:48.530 --> 00:44:54.714
silicone solar cells to maintain
the current matching condition and

00:44:54.714 --> 00:44:58.990
so we wouldn't have to
pay that penalty there.

00:45:00.550 --> 00:45:01.220
Someone said,

00:45:01.220 --> 00:45:06.520
is there any work in progress to
utilize graphene as a substrate?

00:45:08.740 --> 00:45:12.885
Graphene is also a pretty good
transparent conductor and

00:45:12.885 --> 00:45:17.560
there are some academic groups
trying to use it as an electrode.

00:45:17.560 --> 00:45:20.200
And it would also, I think,

00:45:20.200 --> 00:45:25.380
dramatically improve
the stability of the solar cell.

00:45:25.380 --> 00:45:27.679
Hardly anything can go through graphene.

00:45:29.340 --> 00:45:36.210
I hope there are advances in
depositing large sheets of graphene.

00:45:36.210 --> 00:45:38.790
I haven't followed the latest on that, but

00:45:38.790 --> 00:45:43.800
I'm not aware of any real practical
way to put down large sheets of it.

00:45:43.800 --> 00:45:47.589
I think you could have
lots of small flakes and

00:45:47.589 --> 00:45:52.873
that's kind of like those carbon
pastes that I briefly alluded

00:45:52.873 --> 00:45:58.373
to that have been used and
they did result in very stable devices.

00:46:01.380 --> 00:46:08.092
Someone's asking about printing and
I think this is just being touched on.

00:46:08.092 --> 00:46:12.554
And at this time,
I don't think it's clear whether it

00:46:12.554 --> 00:46:17.501
should be slot dye coated or
blade coated or gravure coated or

00:46:17.501 --> 00:46:21.870
perhaps even ink jet printed
with an array of novels.

00:46:21.870 --> 00:46:27.006
I think what is for sure is that it should
not be spin coated at industry scale,

00:46:27.006 --> 00:46:31.275
because spin coating doesn't work well for
a very large area and

00:46:31.275 --> 00:46:33.901
it also wastes so much of the solution.

00:46:33.901 --> 00:46:38.940
So we definitely want a printing
technique where almost

00:46:38.940 --> 00:46:43.667
all of the ink does actually
end up in the solar cell.

00:46:43.667 --> 00:46:48.952
And only a few labs
are working on the scale

00:46:48.952 --> 00:46:53.190
up right now to go to larger area.

00:46:53.190 --> 00:46:59.497
There definitely needs to be
a lot more work in that area.

00:47:01.641 --> 00:47:06.465
There's a question,
do I think mechanical versus

00:47:06.465 --> 00:47:11.296
monolithic will be the better design for
tandems?

00:47:11.296 --> 00:47:16.115
So I believe in mechanical and
I have a few reasons for that.

00:47:17.180 --> 00:47:22.174
One of them is,
I was saying how if the spectrum

00:47:22.174 --> 00:47:26.273
changes during the course of the day or

00:47:26.273 --> 00:47:31.780
through the seasons and
the current match is lost,

00:47:31.780 --> 00:47:35.511
you could use circuitry to fix it.

00:47:35.511 --> 00:47:40.695
Another one is it's just
a practical business concern,

00:47:40.695 --> 00:47:45.555
if someone were to make
the high-band gap top cells,

00:47:45.555 --> 00:47:52.680
they could partner with any company
that makes either silicon or SIGs.

00:47:52.680 --> 00:47:56.400
And those companies would not
have to modify their product.

00:47:56.400 --> 00:48:01.590
They wouldn't have to change the texturing
that they used at the top of their cell.

00:48:01.590 --> 00:48:04.840
They wouldn't have to change
their electrode pattern.

00:48:04.840 --> 00:48:10.246
Whereas if you go monolithic, you've gotta
keep, at the top of your silicon, smooth,

00:48:10.246 --> 00:48:16.350
and that's not as easy as it may sound.

00:48:16.350 --> 00:48:18.120
Now, some believe in monolithic.

00:48:18.120 --> 00:48:21.695
When you do that,
you get rid of one of the transparent

00:48:21.695 --> 00:48:25.996
conductors that you need in
the mechanically stacked tandems.

00:48:25.996 --> 00:48:32.616
And each one of those conductors
costs you about 10% of the photons.

00:48:32.616 --> 00:48:35.082
And so, I'm sure, eventually,

00:48:35.082 --> 00:48:40.510
the world record efficiency will
come from a monolithic design.

00:48:40.510 --> 00:48:45.578
But what's best to get a world
record in an academic lab and

00:48:45.578 --> 00:48:51.999
what's most practical for business,
I think are two different things.

00:48:51.999 --> 00:48:57.672
So not surprisingly, people have asked for
the expected time to market.

00:48:57.672 --> 00:49:02.451
It's always the toughest question for
a professor to answer.

00:49:02.451 --> 00:49:08.752
I've certainly never seen anything
progress as rapidly as this has,

00:49:08.752 --> 00:49:13.448
and a lot of times,
the community poses a problem and

00:49:13.448 --> 00:49:18.391
it gets solved in just a matter
of three to 12 months.

00:49:18.391 --> 00:49:22.080
So things are moving forward very,
very rapidly.

00:49:23.290 --> 00:49:29.360
However, I'm anticipating very
serious financing problems.

00:49:31.480 --> 00:49:36.150
All of the startups are having
tremendous difficulty raising capital.

00:49:37.150 --> 00:49:40.570
A lot of investors lost money,

00:49:40.570 --> 00:49:46.440
total of billions of dollars
were lost between 2005 and 2015.

00:49:46.440 --> 00:49:50.521
And so
there's a reluctance right now to invest.

00:49:50.521 --> 00:49:58.160
And I guess if I look forward here, either
some people are going to step up soon and

00:49:58.160 --> 00:50:02.160
invest, or the numbers are just
going to get more and more compelling.

00:50:02.160 --> 00:50:04.910
The efficiencies are going to keep rising.

00:50:04.910 --> 00:50:06.150
The industry standards.

00:50:06.150 --> 00:50:09.100
Stability tests,
I believe, will be passed.

00:50:09.100 --> 00:50:13.710
We may see academics and
national labs start the scale up and

00:50:13.710 --> 00:50:16.050
demonstrate that there are no issues.

00:50:16.050 --> 00:50:19.960
And then hopefully,
investors will be willing to put in

00:50:19.960 --> 00:50:25.360
the tens of millions that will
be needed for a pilot line.

00:50:25.360 --> 00:50:28.204
And then I think things
could proceed very quickly.

00:50:28.204 --> 00:50:35.914
I think one thing some people learned form
the failures of the last decade is that,

00:50:35.914 --> 00:50:41.963
you don't want to have eight to
ten innovations at the same time.

00:50:41.963 --> 00:50:45.248
You don't want to completely
redesign the panel.

00:50:45.248 --> 00:50:48.088
You don't want to get into
a situation where you need

00:50:48.088 --> 00:50:53.800
to build custom tools because you're doing
something that's never been done before.

00:50:53.800 --> 00:50:58.310
And these designs that I talked about
today, they can all be implemented

00:50:58.310 --> 00:51:02.770
with off the shelf tools that you can get
from companies like, Applied Materials.

00:51:03.940 --> 00:51:09.145
And you can even like, in Silicon Valley,
the tools are around and

00:51:09.145 --> 00:51:14.260
again, you could be using used
tools in a pilot line situation.

00:51:14.260 --> 00:51:19.550
And so, in a best case scenario, I think
products could be ready in a few years but

00:51:19.550 --> 00:51:22.190
financing problems might extend that.

00:51:24.080 --> 00:51:24.626
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:51:24.626 --> 00:51:26.600
Well, thank you for
answering those questions.

00:51:26.600 --> 00:51:28.540
And thank you for
those of you who sent them in.

00:51:28.540 --> 00:51:30.220
We had a fantastic number.

00:51:30.220 --> 00:51:32.300
I think we've all learned
a lot more today.

00:51:32.300 --> 00:51:34.498
I want to thank you for
attending the webinar, and

00:51:34.498 --> 00:51:38.018
hope that you'll be able to join us again
in the future for other faculty talks.

00:51:38.018 --> 00:51:39.735
So, enjoy the rest of your day.

